      "content": "XRP New Rules: Could Deposits Be Locked for at Least 60 Seconds? What’s This Design in XRPL Really For?\nThe new XRPL version code may require deposits to be locked for at least 60 seconds, but it hasn’t been activated yet.\n\nJust added in version 3.4.0, the XRPL Foundation has added a feature: depositing funds may need to be locked for at least 60 seconds, and for no longer than 30 years. NS3.AI spotted the code, but this feature hasn’t been approved and launched yet, so it’s not certain it will be usable. In plain terms, XRPL is trying to introduce a deposit scheme similar to a closed vault, but the exact way it will be used still needs to wait.\n\nImpact on the market\n- Short term: This news itself is unlikely to directly cause XRP to surge or crash. Much depends on how the crypto community interprets it. But if it does roll out, it could make people who want to enter and exit XRP quickly feel it’s inconvenient.\n- Medium term: If this design truly gets implemented, it may make XRP depositors more willing to hold long-term, which could provide slight support to XRP’s price. But it could also discourage players who want to use XRP for short-term trading.\n\nMy take\nI think this design will have little impact on market sentiment in the short term. It mainly depends on how XRPL promotes and executes it. If they can clearly explain the benefits to users—such as higher interest or better stability—it could be somewhat positive. But if depositors feel it’s restrictive, they might choose other chains instead. If I’m wrong, feel free to be gentle—I’ll probably just try a small position.\n\n$BTC $ETH #BTC #ETH\n\n⚠️ This does not constitute investment advice",
